                    <div class="column-content">
                        <div class="need-block">
                            <span class="label label-need">NEED 1</span>
                            <div class="need-text">Amelia presents as a socially and emotionally vulnerable young person with a complex and highly disordered profile of non-verbal communication and social interaction skills associated with her underlying diagnoses of Autistic Spectrum Disorder, Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder and complex medical and mental health needs.</div>
                            <span class="label label-outcome">Outcomes</span>
                            <div class="outcome-text"><strong>E1(i):</strong> <del>On a daily basis, Amelia will be able to independently initiate conversations with both trusted adults and peers across diverse environments, such as educational and social settings. This interaction will encompass not only casual conversation but also the proactive seeking of assistance or clarification through questions.</del><br><br><strong><em>SMART Revision: By [end of term/specific date], Amelia will independently initiate at least [3] conversations per day with trusted adults and/or peers in educational settings, increasing from current baseline of [X] initiations per day. Initiations will include both social conversation (at least [1] per day) and help-seeking/clarification requests (at least [1] per day), as tracked by daily observation logs. Success will be measured by frequency of initiations, independence level (unprompted), and range of communication partners (minimum [2] different adults and [2] different peers per week).</em></strong><br><br><strong>E1(ii):</strong> <del>Amelia will be able to observe examples of social interaction between others (using video, cartoon strips and scripts) and identify/explain at least 2 positive and 2 negative behaviours which support or hinder the success of the communication.</del><br><br><strong><em>SMART Revision: By [end of term/specific date], when presented with [10] video/visual examples of social interactions, Amelia will correctly identify and explain at least 2 positive and 2 negative communication behaviours in 8 out of 10 scenarios (80% accuracy), improving from current baseline of [X/10] correct responses. Explanations must demonstrate understanding of why behaviours support or hinder communication, as assessed by [specific rubric/criteria]. Progress will be reviewed [monthly/termly] using standardized social scenarios.</em></strong><br><br><strong>E2:</strong> <del>Amelia will be able to participate in small group activities with up to three peers who share similar interests and hobbies, for 20 minutes, three times weekly.</del><br><br><strong><em>SMART Revision: By [end of term/specific date], Amelia will successfully participate in small group activities (2-3 peers with shared interests) for 20 minutes, 3 times per week, increasing from current baseline of [X minutes/Y times per week]. Success will be measured by: (1) duration of participation without needing breaks, (2) quality of participation (making at least [2] relevant contributions per session), (3) emotional regulation maintained throughout (no distress episodes), and (4) self-reported enjoyment rating of [X/10] or higher. Progress tracked via weekly observation forms completed by supervising adult.</em></strong></div>
                            <span class="label label-provision">Provision</span>
                            <div class="provision-text"><strong>CRITICAL REVIEW OF NEED 1 PROVISIONS:</strong><br><br><strong>Speech and Language Therapy:</strong><br><strong><em>✓ WHAT is clearly specified:</em></strong> Direct and indirect SLT input for social interaction, non-verbal communication, emotional literacy, and higher-level language skills, relationship building through low-demand activities<br><strong><em>✓ WHO will deliver:</em></strong> Speech and Language Therapist with expertise in complex social communication and emotional needs<br><strong><em>✓ DURATION is clearly specified:</em></strong> 12 hours direct + 15 hours indirect = 27 hours per annum. Session lengths: 30 minutes initially, increasing to 45 minutes. Frequency: 1 session per half term (Terms 1-2), 2 sessions per half term (Terms 3-4), 3 x 45-minute sessions per half term (Terms 5-6)<br><strong><em>✓ WHEN REVIEWED:</em></strong> Half-termly at MDT meetings, formal review at Annual Review<br><br><strong><em>HOWEVER - CRITICAL GAPS IDENTIFIED:</em></strong><br><strong><em>⚠ Missing specificity on WHAT will be targeted in direct sessions beyond "relationship building" - needs breakdown of which outcomes (E1(i), E1(ii), E2) will be addressed in which phases</em></strong><br><strong><em>⚠ Indirect time (15 hours) lacks breakdown - how much for liaison vs training vs reports vs meetings? Currently too vague</em></strong><br><strong><em>⚠ No success criteria specified for when/how session frequency/duration will increase - "as tolerated" is subjective</em></strong><br><br>---<br><br>Amelia will receive direct and indirect speech and language therapy input from a SLT who is experienced in supporting young people with highly complex social communication and emotional needs. Direct input from the Speech and Language Therapist must be flexible and carefully tailored to Amelia's 'readiness' and physical wellbeing (this agreed collectively through liaison/consultation with her parents and other members of the MDT), with the gradual introduction of more frequent/structured contacts (face to face or virtual) working towards 3 x 45-minute sessions per half term by the end of the year. <del>Amelia requires 12 hours of direct Speech and Language Therapy with a specialist SLT spread across the year, this time broken down into sessions of different length and frequency dependent on Amelia's health and wellbeing.</del> <strong><em>Amelia requires 12 hours of direct Speech and Language Therapy with a specialist SLT spread across the year. Initial sessions will be 30 minutes each, increasing to 45 minutes as tolerated. Session frequency will start at 1 session per half term (Term 1-2), increasing to 2 sessions per half term (Term 3-4), and progressing to 3 x 45-minute sessions per half term by end of year (Terms 5-6), adjusted based on Amelia's health and wellbeing.</em></strong> Amelia requires an additional 15 hours of indirect SLT input spread across the year, this time allowing for ad hoc liaison, administrative tasks, the training/support of parents and other professionals (particularly those who are working more regularly with Amelia), the provision of written materials/resources and progress report/s, attendance at the half termly MDT meetings, and attendance at the Annual Review.<br><br>Whilst the longer-term outcome of the Speech and Language Therapy input will be the explicit support of Amelia's social interaction, non-verbal communication, emotional literacy, and higher-level language skills, in the short term the focus must be about building a trusting relationship with Amelia through low-demand activities and informal reciprocal interaction.<br>• Total SLT hours: 27 hours per annum.<br>• <strong><em>Review schedule: Progress reviewed half-termly at MDT meetings, with formal review at Annual Review meeting. Provision to be adjusted based on Amelia's progress, health status, and engagement levels.</em></strong><br><br><strong>Provider:</strong> Speech and language therapist<br><br>---<br><br><strong>LSA Support:</strong><br><strong><em>✓ WHAT is clearly specified:</em></strong> Weekly support to access community/online connections (Music, Crochet, Gaming); targeted social skills support including role playing, social scripts, topic cards, Social Stories, Comic Strip Conversations, teaching about relationships, conversation skills, texting, asking for help<br><strong><em>✓ WHO will deliver:</em></strong> LSA<br><strong><em>✓ DURATION is clearly specified:</em></strong> At least 1 x 40 minute session per week delivered at home or in community<br><strong><em>✓ WHEN REVIEWED:</em></strong> Monthly through parent consultation and LSA logs, formal review at half-termly MDT meetings and Annual Review<br><br><strong><em>HOWEVER - CRITICAL GAPS IDENTIFIED:</em></strong><br><strong><em>⚠ "At least 1x40 minute session a week" is minimum only - no maximum specified. Total LSA hours per annum not stated</em></strong><br><strong><em>⚠ Two distinct activities mentioned (community connections + social skills teaching) but unclear if these occur in same 40-minute session or separately - needs clarification of time allocation</em></strong><br><strong><em>⚠ Resources mentioned (Winner, Schroeder, Kelly) but no indication of who will select, purchase, or train LSA in their use</em></strong><br><strong><em>⚠ "Conversation partners should be attuned" - unclear who these partners are beyond LSA</em></strong><br><br>---<br><br>Amelia will be supported on a weekly basis to access local community or online connections centred around a common ground or interest e.g. Music, Crochet, Gaming which can provide social experiences without conversation. During her one-on-one sessions with her LSA, Amelia must receive targeted social skills support:<br>• This can include role playing exercises, the use of social scripts, topic cards for conversation initiation, and visual aids such as Social Stories or Comic Strip Conversations to clarify social norms and expectations. Teaching about relationships, role modelling, conversation skills, texting, asking for help. Conversation partners should be attuned to Amelia's style of communication and explain ambiguous language, Social communication teaching will ensure Amelia understands how neurotypical individuals communicate.<br>• Use of resources such as Michelle Garcia Winner's Social Thinking or Socially Speaking (Schroeder). Use of resources such as Talkabout (Alex Kelly) or Michelle Garcia Winner's materials. At least 1x40 minute session a week delivered at home or in the community. <strong><em>⚠ RECOMMENDED ADDITION: Specify total LSA hours per annum (minimum 26 hours based on 1x40 mins weekly across 39 school weeks). Clarify time split: e.g., "20 minutes community connection activities + 20 minutes explicit social skills teaching per session, with balance adjusted based on Amelia's capacity and engagement." Specify SLT will provide initial training to LSA on use of specialist resources and ongoing supervision/consultation.</em></strong><br>• <strong><em>Review schedule: LSA social skills support reviewed monthly through parent consultation and LSA logs, with formal review at half-termly MDT meetings and Annual Review.</em></strong><br><br><strong>Provider:</strong> LSA</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="need-block">
                            <span class="label label-need">NEED 2</span>
                            <div class="need-text">Amelia has an uneven/disordered communication profile of stronger and weaker areas, with Amelia's abilities falling between a wide range, from below 1st to 63rd centile. This 'spiky' speech, language and social interaction profile continues to have a significant impact on Amelia as a learner, in addition to significantly affecting her social and emotional well-being.</div>
                            <span class="label label-outcome">Outcomes</span>
                            <div class="outcome-text"><strong><em>No relevant outcome matched to this need. An outcome is needed that specifically addresses supporting Amelia's uneven communication profile and improving consistency across her stronger and weaker areas.</em></strong></div>
                            <span class="label label-provision">Provision</span>
                            <div class="provision-text"><strong><em>MISSING PROVISION - MUST BE ADDED:</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>Differentiated Communication Support:</em></strong><br><strong><em>✓ What: Individualized communication scaffolding that leverages Amelia's stronger areas (e.g., vocabulary knowledge, special interests) to support weaker areas (e.g., pragmatic language, social communication). This includes visual supports, communication scripts, and strength-based learning approaches tailored to her spiky profile.</em></strong><br><strong><em>✓ Who: Speech and Language Therapist (lead), with LSA implementing strategies during daily sessions and tutors applying strategies across curriculum</em></strong><br><strong><em>✓ Session length/frequency: Incorporated into existing SLT sessions (see NEED 1), plus daily implementation by LSA during 40-minute sessions, and ongoing implementation by tutors throughout learning activities</em></strong><br><strong><em>✓ When reviewed: Half-termly at MDT meetings, with communication profile reassessment annually by SLT using standardized assessments to track progress in weaker areas and maintenance of stronger areas</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Specific strategies to include: Use of Amelia's strong vocabulary and science knowledge as foundation for teaching pragmatic skills; visual communication supports (e.g., conversation maps, topic organizers); pre-teaching of social communication expectations before activities; strength-based feedback emphasizing competent areas while gently supporting areas of difficulty; flexible communication expectations adjusted to Amelia's daily capacity based on pain and anxiety levels</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>CRITICAL REVIEW: This provision is COMPREHENSIVE and addresses WHAT/WHO/DURATION/REVIEW. Recommend implementation.</em></strong></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="need-block">
                            <span class="label label-need">NEED 3</span>
                            <div class="need-text">Amelia's communication style varies considerably, dependent on the context in which she is in, her emotional state, and the purpose/theme of the interaction. In situations which Amelia perceives as socially challenging, or where she is feeling anxious, she may limit her contributions (keeping them brief and 'to the point'), withdraw from interaction altogether, or may to try and 'manage' the situation through the introduction of her own pseudo-mature comments and dominant language.</div>
                            <span class="label label-outcome">Outcomes</span>
                            <div class="outcome-text"><strong><em>No relevant outcome matched to this need. An outcome is needed that addresses supporting Amelia's communication across different contexts and emotional states, particularly in reducing withdrawal and anxiety in socially challenging situations.</em></strong></div>
                            <span class="label label-provision">Provision</span>
                            <div class="provision-text"><del>Add provision(s) linked to this need here</del><br><br><strong><em>SUGGESTED PROVISION - Context-Responsive Communication Support:</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ What: Environmental and interactional adaptations to support Amelia's communication across varying contexts and emotional states. This includes: (1) Pre-emptive reduction of social demands when anxiety indicators are observed; (2) Use of low-pressure communication strategies (e.g., parallel activity, side-by-side positioning, written communication options); (3) Explicit recognition and validation of Amelia's need to limit contributions without pressure to participate more; (4) Teaching of graduated exposure strategies to socially challenging situations; (5) Development of "communication passport" detailing Amelia's communication variations across contexts to support all adults working with her</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Who: SLT to develop passport and train staff (2 hours); LSA and tutors to implement daily; Educational Psychologist to support graduated exposure program (linked to anxiety management in SEMH section)</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Session length/frequency: Embedded throughout all interactions with Amelia. LSA dedicates 10 minutes per session to explicit teaching of coping strategies for socially challenging situations (integrated into existing 40-minute weekly LSA sessions from NEED 1). SLT to review and update communication passport termly (1 hour per term = 3 hours annually)</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ When reviewed: Communication passport updated termly by SLT in consultation with Amelia, parents, and staff. Effectiveness of strategies reviewed monthly at LSA supervision and formally at half-termly MDT meetings</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>Rationale: This provision directly addresses the context-dependent nature of Amelia's communication and provides concrete strategies for supporting her across her range of communication presentations, rather than expecting consistent performance</em></strong></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="need-block">
                            <span class="label label-need">NEED 4</span>
                            <div class="need-text">She can become noticeably withdrawn or distressed when faced with social demands.</div>
                            <span class="label label-outcome">Outcomes</span>
                            <div class="outcome-text"><strong><em>No relevant outcome matched to this need. An outcome is needed that focuses on reducing distress and withdrawal when facing social demands, with measurable indicators of Amelia's ability to manage or communicate her needs in these situations.</em></strong></div>
                            <span class="label label-provision">Provision</span>
                            <div class="provision-text"><del>Add provision(s) linked to this need here</del><br><br><strong><em>SUGGESTED PROVISION - Social Demand Management & Early Intervention Support:</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ What: (1) Development and use of a "social demand scale" (0-10) for Amelia to communicate her capacity for social interaction on any given day; (2) Agreement of "safe exit" strategies allowing Amelia to withdraw from overwhelming social situations with dignity (e.g., agreed signals, break cards, time-limited participation with pre-agreed end points); (3) Training for all staff in recognizing early warning signs of withdrawal/distress (e.g., reduced eye contact, monosyllabic responses, body language changes, use of pseudo-mature language); (4) Establishment of low-demand "safe spaces" where Amelia can regulate without social expectations; (5) Daily check-ins using low-demand communication methods (visual scales, written options) to assess social capacity</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Who: SLT to develop social demand scale and train staff in early warning signs (3 hours initial setup); LSA to conduct daily check-ins and support use of safe exit strategies; All tutors and educational staff to implement across all interactions</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Session length/frequency: Daily check-ins with LSA (5 minutes at start of each contact); immediate access to safe exit strategies as needed throughout the day; staff training on early warning signs delivered in first half-term with refresher at mid-year MDT meeting (30 minutes)</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ When reviewed: Daily monitoring through LSA logs tracking frequency of withdrawal episodes, social demand ratings, and use of exit strategies. Formal review monthly with parents and half-termly at MDT meetings to assess patterns and adjust supports</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>Rationale: Based on Amelia's ASD profile and anxiety difficulties (see SEMH section), this provision prioritizes prevention of distress through early recognition and response, while respecting Amelia's autonomy and need for control. This is an essential safeguarding measure given her vulnerability.</em></strong></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="need-block">
                            <span class="label label-need">NEED 5</span>
                            <div class="need-text">Amelia's efficacy as a communicator is significantly influenced by her physical and mental well-being. On days when she experiences heightened levels of pain or anxiety, her ability to articulate herself clearly can be compromised.</div>
                            <span class="label label-outcome">Outcomes</span>
                            <div class="outcome-text"><strong><em>No relevant outcome matched to this need. An outcome is needed that addresses maintaining communication abilities during periods of heightened pain or anxiety, possibly including alternative communication methods or supports.</em></strong></div>
                            <span class="label label-provision">Provision</span>
                            <div class="provision-text"><del>Add provision(s) linked to this need here</del><br><br><strong><em>SUGGESTED PROVISION - Tiered Communication System Based on Wellbeing:</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ What: Multi-modal communication system that adjusts to Amelia's daily wellbeing status: (1) TIER 1 (Good days): Standard verbal communication expectations as per other provisions; (2) TIER 2 (Moderate pain/anxiety): Reduced verbal demands with augmentative supports (e.g., written responses accepted, visual choice boards, text/email communication for non-urgent matters, extended processing time, shorter utterance expectations); (3) TIER 3 (High pain/anxiety): Minimal verbal communication required - use of visual pain/wellbeing scales, yes/no cards, thumbs up/down, text-based communication, pre-prepared communication cards for common needs. All adults trained to recognize which tier Amelia is operating in and adjust expectations accordingly without requiring Amelia to advocate for reduced demands</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Who: SLT to design tiered system and create communication tools/resources (4 hours initial development, 1 hour per term for updates = 7 hours annually); SLT to train all staff in recognizing wellbeing indicators and implementing appropriate tier (2 hours initial training, 30 minutes refresher per term); All educational staff to implement daily; Parents to share wellbeing information as appropriate</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Session length/frequency: Implemented throughout all daily interactions. Communication tier assessed at start of each contact (integrated into daily check-ins from NEED 4). SLT reviews communication tools termly and adjusts based on Amelia's feedback and staff observations</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ When reviewed: Daily monitoring of which tier is being used and its effectiveness via LSA logs. Weekly consultation between LSA and parents regarding patterns. Formal review of system effectiveness at half-termly MDT meetings with adjustments made based on Amelia's feedback, parent input, and staff observations</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>Rationale: Amelia's complex medical needs (chronic pain) and diagnosed anxiety disorders mean her communication capacity is genuinely variable. This provision removes the burden from Amelia to constantly explain her difficulties while ensuring she can still communicate her needs effectively across all wellbeing states. This is CRITICAL for safeguarding as communication breakdown during high pain/anxiety periods could prevent Amelia from accessing help</em></strong></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="need-block">
                            <span class="label label-need">NEED 6</span>
                            <div class="need-text">Amelia non-verbal communication skills are disordered, consistent with her diagnosis of Autism. She can find staying on topic difficult and prefers her own subjects of interest. Amelia's ability to communicate her emotions are significantly delayed. She has social communication difficulties which includes poor theory of mind, conversation initiation and reciprocation, reading of body language, social etiquette, maintaining a balance of dialogue, keeping to topic, showing awareness of the role of others when interacting, and conveying appropriate emotion/feeling in her output. Amelia is reluctant to communicate with new people and prefers the company of those older than her.</div>
                            <span class="label label-outcome">Outcomes</span>
                            <div class="outcome-text"><strong>E1(iv):</strong> <del>Amelia will be able to consistently engage in balanced conversation or discussion with 1 or 2 people (tutors, peers, other professionals) on a daily basis for at least 6 minutes, keeping to topic and making appropriate contributions. Amelia will demonstrate increased confidence in her own communication skills with both familiar and unfamiliar people through the use of a self-rating scale/monitoring system (i.e. using a numbered scale of 0-10 to represent her feelings of communicative confidence and motivation in different contexts/situations).</del><br><br><strong><em>SMART Revision: By [end of term/specific date], Amelia will engage in balanced conversations with 1-2 people (tutors, peers, professionals) for at least 6 minutes, [5] times per day, increasing from current baseline of [X times/Y minutes per day]. Success will be measured by: (1) maintaining on-topic discussion for 80% of conversation time, (2) making balanced contributions (speaking for 30-50% of total conversation time), (3) demonstrating [3] appropriate conversation skills per session (e.g., turn-taking, appropriate eye contact/body orientation, responding to partner's comments), and (4) self-rating communication confidence improving from baseline average of [X/10] to [Y/10] across familiar and unfamiliar partners. Conversations will be tracked daily via observation logs and confidence ratings recorded [weekly/daily].</em></strong><br><br><em>Note: This outcome is weak as it only addresses conversation duration and topic maintenance, but does not adequately address the broader range of social communication difficulties including theory of mind, reading body language, social etiquette, or communicating emotions. More comprehensive outcomes are needed for these areas.</em></div>
                            <span class="label label-provision">Provision</span>
