tion/instruction at a time; (3) Visual supports to aid processing; (4) Normalizing asking for repetition or clarification; (5) Teaching Amelia to recognize which "mode" she is in and use appropriate compensatory strategy</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Who: All educational staff trained in Strategies A, B, C (integrated into Term 1 staff training, total 2 hours). SLT teaches Amelia metacognitive strategies (Strategy B: Stop-Think-Respond, Strategy C: mode recognition) within existing 38 hours allocation (approximately 2 hours across the year). LSA provides practice with strategies using structured activities (10 minutes weekly integrated into existing sessions). Tutors implement throughout daily interactions</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ Session length/frequency: Staff implementation is continuous across all interactions. SLT strategy teaching occurs across approximately 2 hours annually (4-6 sessions). LSA provides 10 minutes weekly practice. Staff training in Term 1 (2 hours, integrated into broader communication training)</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>✓ When reviewed: Staff and LSA track instances of misunderstandings due to impulsive responding and instances of delayed responding in weekly logs, noting context and strategies used. Review at half-termly MDT: (1) Are staff consistently providing extended wait time? (2) Is Amelia using Stop-Think-Respond strategy? (3) Frequency of misunderstandings decreasing? (4) Patterns in when delays vs impulsivity occur to inform when to use which strategy. SLT reviews strategy use and effectiveness termly</em></strong><br><br><strong><em>Rationale: NEED 12 describes processing and responding challenges related to Amelia's anxiety (delays) and ADHD (impulsivity). Both are neurologically-based and will not be "cured" but can be managed through environmental accommodation and strategy teaching. This provision provides adults with tools to support both response patterns while teaching Amelia awareness and compensatory 